---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
const UPDATED = "October 15, 2026";
---

<Page title="Privacy Policy" description="What data Airdrop processes, why, for how long and your rights.">
  <article class="prose-page">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>Last updated: {UPDATED}</p>
    <p>
      This policy explains what personal data Airdrop processes, why, and your rights. It is written with the EU/UK GDPR and
      Brazil's LGPD in mind. Airdrop is designed to need as little data as possible: it never needs to know who your app's
      users are.
    </p>

    <h2>Two kinds of data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>About you, our customer</strong> (people who request access or use the CLI/dashboard): we are the controller.</li>
      <li><strong>About your app's users</strong> (devices that check for updates): you are the controller and we act as your processor, only on your instructions and only to provide the Service.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What we process</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Why</th><th>Kept for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Early-access form: email, app name, size bucket</td><td>To contact you and onboard you (your request / our legitimate interest)</td><td>Until onboarding is done or you ask us to delete it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App tokens (stored only as SHA-256 hashes), app names, plan, signing public key</td><td>To provide the Service (contract)</td><td>While your app exists</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Your uploaded bundles, assets and release notes</td><td>To deliver updates (contract)</td><td>While your app exists; unpublished files are deleted after 7 days</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Per device: a random install ID generated by the SDK, platform, runtime version, channel, current update ID, SDK version</td><td>To choose the right update and roll out gradually (on your behalf)</td><td>Not stored by request; see the next rows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Monthly active-device records: a one-way hash of app + install ID per month</td><td>Plan limits and usage (on your behalf)</td><td>3 months</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Update events: counts of downloads, installs, rollbacks and errors per update</td><td>Release health in the CLI/dashboard (on your behalf)</td><td>Aggregated counts, while the update exists</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IP addresses and request metadata</td><td>Delivery, security and abuse prevention (rate limits)</td><td>Handled by Cloudflare and kept in short-lived logs; not stored in our database</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      We don't collect names, emails, phone numbers, advertising IDs, location or the content users see in your app, and we
      don't sell data or use it for advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it lives and who helps us</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare, Inc.</strong>: hosting, global delivery, database (D1) and file storage (R2). Data may be processed in any country of Cloudflare's network, under Cloudflare's data processing terms and standard contractual clauses.</li>
      <li><strong>GitHub, Inc.</strong>: source code, automation, and internal notifications about early-access requests.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Database backups are kept for 35 days and then deleted automatically.</p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      You can ask to access, correct, export or delete your data, or object to its processing. Customers can delete an app and
      all of its data at any time (<code>DELETE /v1/apps/:id</code>). Requests from your app's users should go to you, the
      controller; we'll help you answer them. Because install IDs are random and stored hashed, we usually cannot tie a record
      to a specific person.
    </p>

    <h2>Security</h2>
    <p>
      Data is encrypted in transit; tokens are stored hashed; customers are isolated per app; updates can be end-to-end
      signed so that not even we can alter what your users run. See <a href="/docs/security/">Security</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes and contact</h2>
    <p>
      We'll post changes here and notify customers of material changes. Privacy questions or requests: contact us through your
      onboarding email or the <a href="/#access">early-access form</a>. You may also complain to your data protection
      authority (in Brazil, the ANPD).
    </p>
  </article>
</Page>
